4139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363271 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363278 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363396 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363403 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363410 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363950 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363953 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121577 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363376 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124835 1071 "Identify the surface $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} =-1$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124835 1071 "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124835 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperboloid of two sheets with $a=b=c=1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121874 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363966 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124884 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $((x - 1)^{2})/(8) - \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124884 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124884 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines in the $xz$-plane." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122144 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363993 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124929 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z - 3 = ((x - 5)^{2})/(5) - \\frac{y^{2}}{4}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124929 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124929 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{25}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =5-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-8 & =-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-4(z-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122636 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364103 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125011 1071 "Given that $f(x, y) = \\arctan (\\frac{x}{y})$, find the set of values $(x, y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125011 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125011 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $y=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}}{1+{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is not defined at the point $(0,0).$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122966 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364115 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125066 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 16 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -7 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & a \\\\ 2 & 0 & b \\\\ 1 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125066 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 16 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & a \\\\ 2 & 0 & b \\\\ 1 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $D = P^{-1}AP$, find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125066 1074 "Notice that $D={P}^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\n\nThe third column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-\\lambda I,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 16 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -7 & 7\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 16 & -14 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 16 & -14 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{8}(7{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(7{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}) \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the third column of $P,$ we deduce that ${x}_{3}=0.$ Now, setting ${x}_{2}=8$ and substituting our values for ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{8}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123254 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364135 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125114 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nDetermine whether $A = PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D$, where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125114 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125114 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128997 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, where\n\n![](images/d9/d96ec9ab40f8f1cadd6d2492dddcc0090f766b72e9c7fdb659e3b2f7f39fce0d.png)\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186128997 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, where\n\n![](images/fe/feda4016e31449244cf758fe239e49c6dd384a91b216763f72210b3c1b213827.png)\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129134 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364121 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129135 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363180 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362844 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364235 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362823 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362830 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362837 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364242 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362816 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364228 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362872 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364297 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364332 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362879 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362886 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364311 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364339 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129172 1038 #edb/r