z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{-1/2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the surface $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127105 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364109 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129609 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129609 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 1$ and $λ_{2} =-4$ of the matrix $A$, given above. Find the third eigenvector $v_{3} = [a, b, c]^{T}$ such that the set ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129609 1074 "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7(\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127213 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364155 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129627 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129627 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(2^{y + 1}x^{2})dxdy$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129627 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{2}^{1+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128581 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364249 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129855 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}6x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0, \\\\ {e}^{x+y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129855 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$, where\n$f(x, y) = {6x,\\begin{vmatrix}if0 < x < 1,-1 < y < 0, \\\\ e^{x + y},\\end{vmatrix}if0 < x < 1, 0 < y < 1$,\nand $R$ is the domain of $f$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129855 1074 "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(0,1)\\times (-1,0)$ and ${R}_{2}=(0,1)\\times (0,1).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}6x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}6x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}({e}^{x})({e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(0-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{1}6x\\,\\text{d}x+({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}+[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+(e-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-2e+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364649 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363222 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364587 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364628 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362991 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362998 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363005 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363012 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363019 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364545 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363033 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364148 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364552 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363040 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363047 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364128 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363054 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363061 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364510 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363089 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363096 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364161 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364538 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363068 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363075 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363082 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363103 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364663 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364635 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364642 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362462 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362475 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364737 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364744 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362635 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362649 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362656 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362642 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130492 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362469 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134061 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134061 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n$p_{1}(t) = 2t, p_{2}(t) = t + 1, p_{3}(t) = 2t + 1$.\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $R_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as\n$⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ = p(0)q(0) + p(-1)q(-1)$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $p_{1}(t)⊥p_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $p_{1}(t)⊥̸p_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t), p_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134061 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(2t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =2(0)\\cdot (0+1)+2(-1)\\cdot ((-1)+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t,2t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2t){|}_{0}\\cdot (2t+1){|}_{0}+(2t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2t+1){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =2(0)\\cdot (2(0)+1)+2(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)+1) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130594 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362587 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134078 1071 "Let $A$ be a $m \\cdot n$ matrix. What is the image of $Col(A)$ under the action of $A^{T}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134078 1071 "Let $A$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of ${A}^{T}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134078 1074 "First,  notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nNow, pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+⋯+{x}_{m}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the column space of ${A}^{T},$ or equivalently, the row space of $A.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}(({A}^{T})^{T})=\\text{Col}(A)$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca8508d05e28e4c91b5a7f3c65826ce68b5dacd382bfb1a76f09c09d1d004bf.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131887 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364168 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134313 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y = 2x$ and $y = x^{2}$, then $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA =$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134313 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2},$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134313 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/c8/c8cf45693dbf89b4f2b5efda32904d07d8205770419cc5ffdec63f4b43c0e145.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =2x \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/14/14d0f60200b6efee0de1611a51a71e27eb6070697cb02db58007b52a8b3f172d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131965 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364174 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134326 1071 "![](images/4d/4d66848f6030504013f8670ab0c8885b5276c833f2e420151e146fcff823ba6f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y = 3 - x$, $y = 0$, and $x = 0$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)ye^{x}dA =$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134326 1071 "![](images/4d/4d66848f6030504013f8670ab0c8885b5276c833f2e420151e146fcff823ba6f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=3-x,$ $y=0,$ and $x=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134326 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-x & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/47/479da73ff2382e584ec7eb02ff2517c7fa21322faf5ecbda2d2ff2d65049ef31.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132229 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364180 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134370 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134370 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n${2x_{1} + x_{2} = 5; x_{1} - x_{2} = 2; - 3x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 4$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134370 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133344 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364690 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134556 1071 "Given that $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$, find $⟨a, b - (1 + 3\\textrm{i})c⟩$ if\n\n$⟨b, a⟩ = 1 + \\textrm{i}$ and $⟨a, c⟩ = 2$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134556 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=2.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134556 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩-⟨a,(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{1+\\text{i}}-(1-3\\text{i})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}-2+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =-1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151810 1071 "$\\qquad$ $P(n)$: $\\dfrac{n}{3} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ or $\\sqrt{n}\\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{N}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The truth set of $P(n)$ is $\\emptyset$.\n2. The truth set of $P(n)$ is $\\mathbb{N}$.\n3. $\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151810 1071 "$\\qquad$ $P(n):$ $\\dfrac{n}{3} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ or $\\sqrt{n}\\in \\mathbb{N}.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{N},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $\\emptyset.$\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $\\mathbb{N}.$\n\n- $\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151810 1074 "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x)$, defined over the universal set $U$, transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x)$.\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U$, i.e. if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U{:}$\n$T_P = U$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\dfrac{n}{3} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$\" is $\\mathbb{N}$, and\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{n}\\in \\mathbb{N}$\" is $\\{n=m^2: m \\in \\mathbb{N} \\} = \\{1,4,9,16, 25, \\ldots \\}$.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT_P = \\mathbb{N} \\cup \\{1,4,9,16, 25, \\ldots \\} =\\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $T_P= \\mathbb{N}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $T_P$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151810 1074 "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U{:}$\n\n$$\nT_P = U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\dfrac{n}{3} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$\" is $\\mathbb{N},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{n}\\in \\mathbb{N}$\" is $\\{n=m^2 : m \\in \\mathbb{N} \\} = \\{1,4,9,16, 25, \\ldots \\}.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\nT_P = \\mathbb{N} \\cup  \\{1,4,9,16, 25, \\ldots \\} =\\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $T_P= \\mathbb{N}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T_P$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151811 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x)$: $x^2 \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and $x \\geq 0$\n\nGiven that this predicate is defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{Z}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The truth set of $P(x)$ is $\\emptyset$.\n2. The truth set of $P(x)$ is $\\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n3. $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(x)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151811 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x):$ $x^2 \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and $x \\geq 0$\n\nGiven that this predicate is defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{Z},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $\\emptyset.$\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $\\mathbb{Z}^+.$\n\n- $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(x).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151811 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x)$, defined over the universal set $U$, transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x)$.\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U$, i.e., if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\nT_P \\neq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x^2 \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" is the set $\\mathbb{Z}\\setminus \\{0\\}$, and\n- the truth set of \"$x \\geq 0$\" is $\\mathbb{Z}_{0}^+$.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT_P = \\big(\\mathbb{Z}\\setminus \\{0\\} \\big) \\cap \\mathbb{Z}_{0}^+ = \\mathbb{Z}^+\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $T_P = \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $T_P$ is *not* empty, the statement $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151811 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e., if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\nT_P \\neq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x^2 \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" is the set $\\mathbb{Z}\\setminus \\{0\\},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$x \\geq 0$\" is $\\mathbb{Z}_{0}^+.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT_P = \\big(\\mathbb{Z}\\setminus \\{0\\} \\big) \\cap \\mathbb{Z}_{0}^+ = \\mathbb{Z}^+.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $T_P = \\mathbb{Z}^+.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T_P$ is *not* empty, the statement $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151812 1071 "$\\qquad$ $\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\,$ $\\pi x$ is irrational\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?\n\n- There are rational numbers such that $\\pi x$  is irrational.\n\n- The product of any rational number and $\\pi$ is irrational\n\n- There exists a rational number $x$ such that $\\pi x$ is rational." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151812 1071 "$\\qquad$ $\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\,$ $\\pi x$ is irrational\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?\n\n1. There are rational numbers such that $\\pi x$ is irrational.\n2. The product of any rational number and $\\pi$ is irrational\n3. There exists a rational number $x$ such that $\\pi x$ is rational." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151813 1071 "$\\qquad$ $\\exists\\, a \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\sqrt{a} >    a^2.$ \n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?\n\n- There does not exist a real number whose square root is greater than its square.\n\n- For every real number, its square root is greater than its square.\n\n- There exists a real number whose square root is greater than its square" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151813 1071 "$\\qquad$ $\\exists\\, a \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\sqrt{a} > a^2$.\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?\n\n1. There does not exist a real number whose square root is greater than its square.\n2. For every real number, its square root is greater than its square.\n3. There exists a real number whose square root is greater than its square" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [175921