"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151818 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n\\forall\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\exists y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ For every real $x$ there exists real $y$ such that $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $2+y=0$ for some real number $y$ (e.g., for $y=-2$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(x): \\quad \\exists \\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0\n$$\n\nis true for any real $x.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1): \\quad \\exists \\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, 1 + y=0\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2): \\quad \\exists \\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, 2+ y=0\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $y=-2.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n\\big( \\, \\forall\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\exists y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0 \\, \\big)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\, \\big( \\, \\underbrace{\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0}_{P(x)} \\, \\big)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $\\mathbb R.$ And, according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151819 1071 "$$\n\\big( \\, \\forall \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\text{I}}}{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \n {x} \n> 1 \\, \\big) \\Rightarrow \\big( \\, \\exists \n{y} \n\\in [0, 2\\pi), \\, \n\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\text{II}}}{x} \\!\n\\cos\\!\\!{\\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[2pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\text{III}}}{y}} \\!=1 \\, \\big)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151819 1071 "$\\big(\\, \\forall \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\text{I}}}{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, {x} > 1 \\, \\big) \\Rightarrow \\big(\\, \\exists {y} \\in [0, 2\\pi), \\, \\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\text{II}}}{x} \\! \\cos\\!\\!{\\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[2pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\text{III}}}{y}} \\!=1 \\, \\big)$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151819 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{\\small\\text{b}})$ or free $(\\bbox[2pt, LightBlue]{\\small\\text{f}})$.\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall}{:}$\n$\\big(\\,\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{x} \\!\\! \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x > 1 \\, \\big) \\Rightarrow \\big(\\, {\\exists} {y} \\in [0, 2\\pi), \\, x\\cos y =1\\, \\big)$\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x$.\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $\\exists y$ that uses $y{:}$\n$∃y$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\big(\\, {\\forall} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{x} \\!\\! \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{x} \\!\\! > 1 \\, \\big) \\Rightarrow \\big(\\, {\\exists} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{y} \\