6045585 false] [17592186152006 1074 "A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is a injection (also known as one-to-one) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y$.\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1, x_2 \\in X$, if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$, we have $x_1=x_2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given functions.\n\n- I is *not* an injection. For example, $f(0) = 1 = f(2\\pi)$, but $0 \\neq 2\\pi$.\n- II is an injection. Setting $g(x_1) = g(x_2)$, we get the following:\n$g(x_{1})|= g(x_{2}); 3x_{1} + 2|= 3x_{2} + 2; 3x_{1}|= 3x_{2}; x_{1}|= x_{2}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152006 1074 "A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is a injection (also known as one-to-one) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1, x_2 \\in X,$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2),$ we have $x_1=x_2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given functions.\n\n- I is *not* an injection. For example, $f(0) = 1 = f(2\\pi),$ but $0 \\neq 2\\pi.$\n\n- II is an injection. Setting $g(x_1) = g(x_2),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2 & =3{x}_{2}+2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152007 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2$?\n\n1. The range of $f$ is $[-2,\\infty)$\n2. $\\forall\\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n3. $f$ is **onto**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152007 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-2,\\infty)$\n\n- $\\forall\\, y \\in \\mathbb{R},$ $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- $f$ is **onto**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152007 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/eb/ebc3b7dee4bd542d86580ac1fd442c3e5cdb193306dadbdd15c8f3a07cb26a35.png)\n\nA function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y$, there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y$, $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2$, and it occurs at the point $(1,-2)$. The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=-2$ and greater, including the number $-2$, so the range of the function is $[-2,\\infty)$.\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=-3$. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3$. So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$, there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y$.\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection (also known as *onto*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152007 1074 "First, we graph the function.  \n\n![](images/eb/ebc3b7dee4bd542d86580ac1fd442c3e5cdb193306dadbdd15c8f3a07cb26a35.png)\n\nA function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y,$ $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=-2$ and greater, including the number $-2,$ so the range of the function is $[-2,\\infty).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=-3.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $\\mathbb{R},$ there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection  (also known as *onto*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152008 1071 "![](images/fd/fdbe8197a835c8b1778068ffbafc9e954061485f61b3af5438a98a989e79bf1a.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,\\infty)\\to[-1, \\infty)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty)$\n\n- $\\forall\\, y \\in [-1,\\infty),$ $\\exists\\, x \\in [0,\\infty)$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- $f$ is **onto**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152008 1071 "![](images/fd/fdbe8197a835c8b1778068ffbafc9e954061485f61b3af5438a98a989e79bf1a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,\\infty)\\to[-1, \\infty)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty)$\n2. $\\forall\\, y \\in [-1,\\infty)$, $\\exists\\, x \\in [0,\\infty)$ such that $f(x)=y$\n3. $f$ is **onto**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152008 1074 "A function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y$, there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y$, $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As we can see from the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-1$ and the graph covers all $y$-values greater than or equal to $1$, so the range of the function is $[-1, \\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-1, \\infty)$, it follows that for all $y$ contained within $[-1, \\infty)$, there exists some $x$ contained within $[0, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) = y$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is indeed a surjection of $[0,\\infty)$ onto $[-1, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152008 1074 "A function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y,$ $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As we can see from the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-1$ and the graph covers all $y$-values greater than or equal to $1$, so the range of the function is $[-1, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-1, \\infty)$, it follows that  for all $y$ contained within $[-1, \\infty),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[0, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) = y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is indeed a surjection of $[0,\\infty)$ onto $[-1, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152009 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=x^2$?\n\n1. $f(-1) = f(1)$\n2. $\\forall\\, x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$, we have $x_1=x_2$\n3. $f$ is an injection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152009 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=x^2?$\n\n- $f(-1) = f(1)$\n\n- $\\forall\\, x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2),$ we have $x_1=x_2$\n\n- $f$ is an injection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152009 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a7/a7cf66d034f2394c019de7c