n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $T$ | $T$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $T$ | $F$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $F$ | $T$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $F$ | $F$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152472 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,¬(P∨Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152472 1074 "First, we add a column for $P∨Q.$ Remember that $P∨Q$ is true if either $P$ is true or $Q$ is true.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,P∨Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $¬(P∨Q),$ where the truth values are opposite to the corresponding values in the column for $P∨Q.$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,P∨Q\\,$ | $\\,¬(P∨Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150905 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364360 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152738 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152738 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n1. $f: R → (0, ∞)$ defined by $f(x) = 3^{x}$\n2. $g: Q → Q$ defined by $g(x) = x^{2}$\n3. $h: R → R$ defined by $h(x) = x^{2}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152738 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151115 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364366 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152773 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- $g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152773 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n1. $f: {0, 1, 2} → {1, 3, 9, 27}$ defined by $f(x) = 3^{x}$\n2. $g: [0, ∞) → R$ defined by $g(x) = x^{3}$\n3. $h: {0, 1, 2} → {-1, 1, 3}$ defined by $h(x) = 2x - 1$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152773 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the range. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151451 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364407 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152829 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $y = 1 - x^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. If $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$, and\n$∬_(D)(xy + y^{2})dA = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. ${xy + y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∉ D$\n2. ${xy + y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∉ R$\n3. ${xy + y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152829 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152829 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/66/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157013 1071 "Express the following sentence in formal language.\n\n> The square of a natural number is also a natural number." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157013 1071 "Express the following sentence in formal language.\n\nThe square of a natural number is also a natural number." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157013 1074 "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as:\n\n> *For any natural number $n$, we have that $n^2$ is also a natural number.*\n\nNow, \"$n^2$ is also a natural number\" means:\n\n> *There exists a natural number $m$ such that $n^2 = m$.*\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\n> *For any natural number $n$, there exists another natural number $m$ such that $n^2 = m$.*\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$$\n\\forall \\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists \\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, n^2 = m\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157013 1074 "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as: \n\n*For any natural number $n,$ we have that $n^2$ is also a natural number.*\n\nNow, \"$n^2$ is also a natural number\" means:\n\n*There exists a natural number $m$ such that $n^2 = m.$*\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\n*For any natural number $n,$ there exists another natural number $m$ such that $n^2 = m.$*\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$$\n\\forall \\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists \\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, n^2 = m.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157014 1071 "Express the following statement in formal language.\n\n> For each natural number, there is an integer such that their sum is $2$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157014 1071 "Express the following statement in formal language.\n\nFor each natural number, there is an integer such that their sum is $2.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157014 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"each\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $\\forall$.\n- The words \"there is\" indicate that we use an existential quantifier, $\\exists$.\n- The sentence refers to a natural number and an integer number. So, by denoting our numbers as $n$ and $k$, respectively, we have\n$\\forall \\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists \\, k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n$n + k = 2$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n> $\\forall \\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists \\, k \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, n + k = 2$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157014 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"each\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $\\forall.$\n\n- The words \"there is\" indicate that we use an existential quantifier, $\\exists.$\n\n- The sentence refers to a natural number and an integer number. So, by denoting our numbers as $n$ and $k,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\forall \\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists \\, k \\in \\mathbb{Z}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nn + k = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$\\forall \\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists \\, k \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, n + k = 2$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157015 1071 "Express the following statement in formal language:\n\n> There is a prime $p$ that is even." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157015 1071 "Express the following statement in formal language:\n\nThere is a prime $p$ that is even." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157015 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The words \"there is\" indicate we use an existential quantifier, $\\exists$.\n- The sentence refers to the prime numbers. So, by denoting a prime number as $p$, we have that\n$p$\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as\n$p \\textrm{is even.}$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n> $\\exists$ $p$ prime, $p$ is even.\n\nWe could also write\n\n> $\\exists$ $p$ prime, $2\\mid p$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157015 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The words \"there is\" indicate we use an existential quantifier, $\\exists.$\n\n- The sentence refers to the prime numbers. So, by denoting a prime number as $p,$ we have that \n![](images/36/3680e42982fa38c96aac7e1c0730f8588c4d58ae4036332a255eadc27d60b676.png)\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as\n\n$$\np \\textrm{ is even.}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$\\exists$ $p$ prime, $p$ is even.\n\nWe could also write\n\n$\\exists$ $p$ prime, $2\\mid p.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363680 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363693 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362809 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364566 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364656 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363194 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363201 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363208 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363537 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363888 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363544 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363557 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363825 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363846 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363853 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153986 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363551 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157630 1071 "> There is a prime $p$ such that one more than its double is also prime.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157630 1071 "There is a prime $p$ such that one more than its double is also prime.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157630 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The words \"there is\" indicate we use an existential quantifier, $∃.$\n\n- The sentence refers to prime numbers. So, by denoting a prime number as $p,$ we have that \n\n$$\n∃\\,p\\text{ prime}.\n$$\n\n- \"One more than its double\" can be written as $2p+1,$ so the condition in the statement can be written as\n\n$$\n2p+1\\text{ is prime.}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∃\\,p$ prime, $2p+1$ is prime." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154076 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363902 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157641 1071 "> There is a rational number such that, for any rational number, the sum of both numbers is the second number.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157641 1071 "There is a rational number such that, for any rational number, the sum of both numbers is the second number.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157641 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The words \"there is\" indicate that we use an existential quantifier, $∃.$\n\n- The word \"any\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to two rational numbers. So, by denoting our numbers as $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n∃\\,a\\in Q,\\,∀\\,b\\in Q.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\na+b=b\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∃\\,a\\in Q,\\,∀\\,b\\in Q,\\,a+b=b$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155691 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364490 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157800 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬A∧B$\n- $¬(A∨¬B)$\n- $A∨¬B$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157800 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n1. $¬A∧B$\n2. $¬(A∨¬B)$\n3. $A∨¬B$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157800 1074 "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∨¬B)\\,$ | $\\,A∨¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬A∧B$ and $¬(A∨¬B)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162048 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364011 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162048 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364054 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362593 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362858 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362600 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362607 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362865 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363145 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363236 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363152 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363159 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363229 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161648 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186162731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364731 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162731 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162731 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186162731 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363187 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362580 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363173 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364683 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362781 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362719 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362726 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362733 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362747 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362740 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362754 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362761 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362768 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168585 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186172292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362775 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172292 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x, y) = 2\\cos πxi + 2\\sin (\\frac{πy}{2})j$ at the indicated points?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186172292 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,j$ at the indicated points?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172292 1074 "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(-2,-1),$ and $(4,-4).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\,j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-3))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{2})\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-2,-1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-2))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2})\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-4) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (4))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-4)}{2})\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171357 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186172627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364677 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172627 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172627 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$,\nand the curve $C$, defined as\n$C: 2x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} + 4\\sqrt{5}x_{1} - 2\\sqrt{5}x_{2} =-26$.\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x) = 2x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + 5x_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2}$, what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186172627 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})-2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2}+25)-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2}-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})^{2}}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis defines an empty set, also called an imaginary ellipse. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175951 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362628 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175951 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363166 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175991 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362663 13194139533445 175