predicates: \n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is composite.\n\n- $Q(x):$ $10$ is divisible by $x.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198718 1074 "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={4,6,8,9,10}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} & =\\overset{―}{{4,6,8,9,10}} \\\\  & ={2,3,5,7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{Q}={2,5,10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={2,3,5,7}\\cup {2,5,10} \\\\  & ={2,3,5,7,10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194922 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186198721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198721 1071 "![](images/10/10d3d9a3b026fc1511d9e7a57c1f3276606c9397f0045fdb7748e3038a42fb21.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$, defined over the universal set $R^{2}$. Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186198721 1071 "![](images/10/10d3d9a3b026fc1511d9e7a57c1f3276606c9397f0045fdb7748e3038a42fb21.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198721 1074 "Since we have that \n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5dfcb1cff13cc5f5b6dcfa42a7e1d08184e54a7edd91717b795692068db37121.png)\n\nThen, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}$ and ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/3c/3c4b6cd6b5649a18bd768b3086ccef5a995f9745a9ce90160507c9d92f46f8dc.png)\n\nAs a result, ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}$ is the following: \n\n![](images/62/62373d6926552c34849e91115163dba42483c6259482bc4893704af570749c89.png)\n\nFinally, to get the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$ or ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/25/252c206ca03b339c4bb186c95d21cd26110fc902f0ab9bca6df2d8d594aacba5.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362463 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1069 "q-100120" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1072 #uuid "cb972e63-3e46-543b-9a40-0120a6183656" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (1-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2-t){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-1)+(2-2)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,2-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (2-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362464 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362465 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362466 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362467 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362468 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362466 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1080 #uuid "cdd30202-889c-59ba-bb4d-be2188ca8246" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1