 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362524 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to [-2,2]$ which is given by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+1?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,2]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [-2,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362524 1072 #uuid "18cf57da-d5c7-5b11-9220-be1574e018d6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362524 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/e1/e1a02621cfacc791c1b100b01957195f1f41e9bf78b65286eabbd13363930c67.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $2$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=-1.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-2,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained in $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $[-2,2].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362526 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362527 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362528 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362529 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362530 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1759218636252