Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+I)^{2}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A+I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A+I)v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A+I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A+I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362630 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1