ue] [17592186362634 1084 #uuid "b53bec37-2e64-569a-95cf-491005c8ccae" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362636 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362635 1069 "q-109853" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362635 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$\n- $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362635 1072 #uuid "fd0235ca-04db-545b-b3a6-a1ff38871984" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362635 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ is the left null space of $B.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${B}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$, that is, each column of $B$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362637 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362638 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362639 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362640 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362641 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362636 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362641 13194139533445 1