86045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363178 1084 #uuid "d43b8717-9c04-5178-8d01-d3b35a43962f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363179 1081 "A parabola" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363179 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363179 1084 #uuid "bd261e77-9409-5c92-9d2a-3ddcd21d645b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363181 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363180 1069 "q-140722" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363180 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363180 1072 #uuid "c980d806-f256-5388-b798-65b34624c78e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363180 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )[(3-\\lambda )^{2}-1] & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1759218636318