s:\n\n$\\,$ For all reals $x$ and $y,$ we have $\\sqrt{xy}\\in R.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $\\sqrt{2y}\\in R$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(x)$ is false for all values $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{2y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x$ for any value of $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R\\,(\\,{{∀y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $R.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363862 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363863 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363864 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363865 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363866 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363862 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363861 1080 #uuid "75bd0470-0384-5aff-b357-d583645bbff8" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363862 1081 "None of the statements are true" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363862 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585