ue] [17592186363950 1069 "q-339620" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363950 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{5}+8}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363950 1072 #uuid "d1a99de9-3094-521c-b97f-09f959b1dbb5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363950 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{5}+8} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-x+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-x+0-0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-x \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363951 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363952 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363951 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363951 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363951 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363952 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363951 1080 #uuid "f019cc41-8da7-5024-ae49-0ad52288d3c0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363952 1081 "-\\infty " 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363952 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363952 1084 #uuid "7799bd18-bab3-5b61-a39d-f5541ec35b47" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363954 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363953 1069 "q-339638" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363953 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{-5{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-2}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363953 1072 #uuid "3a3bfe5e-230a-504d-a47d-43d203795529" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363953 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7}{-5{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{-5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(-5+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-5+0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363955 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363954 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363954 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363954 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363955 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363954 1080 #uuid "db44cf64-98a4-5342-8bdb-45dc71ec0667" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363955 1081 "0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363955 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363955 1084 #uuid "18178373-3a85-55b3-b360-e9f08cea2790" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363957 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363956 1069 "q-351874" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363956 1071 "Find the coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363956 1072 #uuid "3d18f814-2b3f-58a2-9c68-5cba48a47c8e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363956 1074 "The coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-1)=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363957 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363958 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363957 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363957 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363957 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363958 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363957 1080 #uuid "b29c3103-a8c8-5a26-be89-4275906c0ecd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363958 1081 "-\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363958 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363958 1084 #uuid "3d652d49-2fb5-5a8c-8c75-c64c8f95ebaf" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363960 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363959 1069 "q-35265" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363959 1071 "Given that the intersection of the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}={z}^{2}$ and the plane $z=3$ is an ellipse, what is the length of its minor axis?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363959 1072 #uuid "567f06ad-7dd7-54b0-b743-ae083d4d749f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363959 1074 "To find the intersection of the elliptic cone and the plane $z=3,$ we substitute $z=3$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{81}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with a semi-minor axis of length $b=3\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore, the minor axis has length $2b=6\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363961 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363962 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363963 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363964 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363965 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363962 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363960 1080 #uuid "66f7cc27-507e-538b-9461-36cbd01fe66d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363961 1081 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363961 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363961 1084 #uuid "b32caa62-88f6-5d6a-8bf1-85854207