, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $4$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $4$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,4].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[0,4]$, it follows that for all $y$ contained within $[0,4],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the range of $f$ is not $[0,5],$ then it's *not true*  that for all $y$ contained within $[0,5],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y.$ In conclusion, $f$ is not a surjection of $[-1,4]$ onto $[0,5].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364415 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364416 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364417 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364418 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364419 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364419 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364414 1080 #uuid "7ab73909-29d3-5746-8a6f-11534ef95f8f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364415 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364415 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364415 1084 #uuid "beed1dde-64a9-5a2f-85c7-a4aa0a67c371" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364416 1081 "I only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364416 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364416 1084 #uuid "f685a086-fb56-54be-97b6-f97431d956fa" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364417 1081 "III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364417 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364417 1084 #uuid "de31e724-cf27-5b01-b706-dc5338e8267b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364418 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364418 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364418 1084 #uuid "7e8ff76c-b7f5-5cfb-b433-00e86f59b9ab" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364419 1081 "II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364419 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364419 1084 #uuid "295605fc-2c98-5de1-86a7-95744cbf3452" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364421 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364420 1069 "q-80297" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364420 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,3]\\to [0,8]$ which is given by $y={2}^{x}?$ \n\n- The range of $f$ is $[1,8]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [0,8],$ $∃\\,x\\in [0,3]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364420 1072 #uuid "5848bc45-4fef-5470-b7b2-022f9067ecb2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364420 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/47/47407d426cf9bb816c0b3dd052138b637bc73e7aa6d751278b20d8add020526d.png)\n\n \nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $8$ and the lowest $y$-value is $1.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $1$ and $8$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is indeed $[1,8].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in [0,3]$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[0,8],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[0,3]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $[0,3]$ onto $[0,8].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364422 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364423 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364424 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364425 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364426 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364424 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364421 1080 #uuid "7902b1b4-c7f8-5523-a027-83ac323d442b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364422 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364422 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364422 1084 #uuid "bc9d9efd-9cf7-5158-9039-0c75b764197a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364423 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364423 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364423 1084 #uuid "7d025f99-60cb-5528-a1e1-fd2e88aa441b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364424 1081 "I only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364424 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364424 1084 #uuid "4d7876f4-0c45-5968-a14f-c9accb6fc96f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364425 1081 "III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364425 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364425 1084 #uuid "b3e24482-eb20-52df-aa73-1e2c1dff6822" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364426 1081 "II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364426 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364426 1084 #uuid "c19d838b-1ff4-51ee-b7be-4b5c2c7e2ba1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364428 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364427 1069 "q-80395" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364427 1071 "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364427 1072 #uuid "e96e8c39-27d3-5b9a-9e75-bf048a578366" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364427 1074 "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364429 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364428 1075 #ed