fa896118eec1e3fcabcde626d9.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}-x & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ 3-2x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-3\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/78/78e2ec78c9fb00fb56b7f84b2a5dbb96786dd07e3057b894ad2421e89ede29e2.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{3}{2}-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2}^{3/2-x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2}^{3/2-x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364547 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364548 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364549 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364550 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364551 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364549 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364546 1080 #uuid "b577663f-37ae-5577-b375-bf9672122434" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364547 1081 "{\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{3/2-x}^{{x}^{2}/2}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364547 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364547 1084 #uui