4139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364694 1084 #uuid "27ad374f-3361-5475-8073-b762bb17dd5f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1081 "5-2\\text{i}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1084 #uuid "14945f42-7413-59f1-969e-cad3bb3baa75" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364697 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1069 "q-96982" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1072 #uuid "7a4d7eeb-44c2-5067-912c-74d45de2e342" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{1}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-3}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-3)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-3})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-3})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364698 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364699 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364700 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364701 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364702 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364699 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1080 #uuid "3e2cb551-0d9b-52a4-a27b-09713a092ec4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364698 1081 "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364698 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364698 1084 #uuid "562651bd-6747-5b13-80ba-21daf53e1cb2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1759