194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364734 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364734 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364734 1084 #uuid "5fa7355e-413d-5cfe-860d-bd5a74b01f14" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364735 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364735 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364735 1084 #uuid "d2048545-37eb-5154-98fb-fbdc70936239" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364736 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364736 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364736 1084 #uuid "a3ccc71c-705b-55f1-bd7a-216a4ecad2d5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364738 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364737 1069 "q-99863" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364737 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364737 1072 #uuid "d0457d3f-7a7d-578e-b127-39bac9e28900" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364737 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{4}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364739 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364740 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364741 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364742 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364743 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364741 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364738 1080 #uuid "7ae35f27-629d-5486-b089-c32b9e81f7a5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364739 1081 "None of the statements are true" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364739 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364739 1084 #uuid "bac9a951-8302-5f23-bf49-b9764766cca8" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364740 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364740 1083 #edb