et of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ differentiating we have $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364746 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364747 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364748 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364749 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364750 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364750 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364745 1080 #uuid "e026abb8-5084-50ac-8a69-bf6908267de3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364746 1081 "None of the statements are true" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364746 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364746 1084 #uuid "a5928cb0-0804-50b0-bb37-632e6f6c8aa0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364747 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364747 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364747 1084 #uuid "87255850-4e57-5447-9045-42b3b82466cf" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364748 1081 "I only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364748 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364748 1084 #uuid "e66a627d-2264-5cb0-93c7-5b9a95b90304" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true